   Abdul-Rahman, Houssam and Albalushi, Alaya and Alakbari, Shaikhah and
   Alsada, Rawan and Alkalbani, Roudha and Al Ghailani, Salma and Masoud,
   Abdul Rahman},
Title = {Simulation-based early detection and classification of severity of
   traffic incident using deep learning and dynamical reservoir features},
Journal = {TRANSPORTATION RESEARCH INTERDISCIPLINARY PERSPECTIVES},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {40},
Month = {NOV},
Abstract = {Accurate and timely detection of traffic incidents is key to improving
   road safety and reducing congestion in urban networks. This paper
   presents a deep learning framework for classifying the severity of
   traffic incidents using high resolution simulation data generated by PTV
   Vissim. A 5 km section of Shaikh Zayed bin Sultan Street in Abu Dhabi
   was modeled with various lane closure scenarios, with traffic data
   collected at 1-second intervals from fixed virtual sensors. Three
   classification schemes were tested: binary (major vs not major),
   three-class (major, minor, normal) and full five-class (0 to 4 lane
   closures) and at different temporal resolutions (1s, 5s, 20s). The data
   was preprocessed into time series sequences while preserving temporal
   dependencies. Classification was done using three models: 1D
   Convolutional Neural Network (1D-CNN), Temporal Convolutional Network
   (TCN) and Reservoir Computing (RC). Results show that the RC model was
   able to detect major incidents within 3-5 min using 1-second data with
   accuracy above 90\% by 10 min. These results show the effectiveness of
   temporal modeling in incident classification and the importance of data
   resolution in early traffic event detection.},
